  "description": "Core AI agent detection engine for .NET. Detects AI agents (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, etc.) via user-agent patterns, header analysis, and behavioral signals. Platform-agnostic — use with any .NET HTTP server, including ASP.NET Core (via Checkpoint.AspNetCore) and classic .NET Framework 4.6.2+ (via Checkpoint.AspNet).",
  "id": "Checkpoint.Core",
  "isPrerelease": false,
  "lastEdited": "2026-10-20T04:00:56.437Z",
  "licenseExpression": "MIT",
  "licenseUrl": "https://licenses.nuget.org/MIT",
  "listed": true,
  "packageHash": "rZLF++7+qRaVhyjNIhlCntqhBbYd8Cf2jK0E2sr/Cedpj58HDK5rtSQmgbi/z60kz+2tIJ7haYt9fPMPaD+GvQ==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 13047676,
  "published": "2026-10-20T03:52:55.397Z",
  "releaseNotes": "1.13.0: security fix for unverified identity headers, signed per-org status lists verified in the engine, fail-closed credential outages, re-stamped OpenAI signing key and a working Instruct fallback link\n\nSecurity fix (#6258): an unverified request header could change how a request\nwas classified and named, and so get it past tenant policy. When the engine had\nnot flagged an agent, both adapters relabelled the request as a named AI agent\nfrom client-supplied headers that nothing verified (a vendor client header, or\na Signature-Agent whose signature did not verify). Composed Cedar\n(principal.category, principal.name) and the dashboard deny list read the\nrelabelled result, so a request could pass a rule written against bots,\nincomplete-data visitors, a named agent or a deny-listed agent. Rules that\nrequire a verified delegation or granted scopes were not affected. The engine's\nclassification now stands. Affects Checkpoint.AspNetCore and Checkpoint.AspNet\n0.2.0 through 1.12.0; the composed-policy bypass affects 1.2.0 through 1.12.0.\nSupplementaryDetector.Apply remains only as an [Obsolete(error: true)] stub\nthat returns the engine's result unchanged: an app still on an older adapter is\nfixed by updating Checkpoint.Core alone, and source that calls it no longer\ncompiles. SignatureVerificationHelper no longer turns an unverified signature\nverifier answer into an AI agent; a custom ISignatureVerifier must return\nVerified = true to change the result.\n\nSigned per-org status lists (#5887, #5894, #6051): the embedded engine now\nverifies every status list against its issuer's DID document (signature,\nassertion key, list URL and validity window) before any bit is used, and there\nis no unsigned path. A credential's status is read only from its issuing org's\nown list, https://<host>/orgs/<slug>/status-lists/<listId>, and a\nlist counts only for credentials of the issuer that signed it. The retired\nplatform-wide and per-org delegation lists are not signed per issuer and never\nverify, so a credential that points at one is not admitted. Status lists are\nrefetched every 30 seconds and never used more than 60 seconds after they were\nfetched (it was 300), nor after the engine's trust window for them ends. A\nstatus index past the end of an org list (131072 entries) is treated as\nrevoked. PreWarmStatusListUrls takes org list URLs. WebMCP read-only requires a\ndid:web org issuer (did:web:<host>:orgs:<slug>) and reads each credential's\nlist from the issuer document's origin.\n\nFail closed on credential outages (#5652): a request presenting a KYA\ncredential that fails verification returns 503 with Retry-After: 3 even when\nFailOpen is enabled. Trusted roots are resolved for KYA signatures,\ndelegation-proof headers (including KYA-OS-Delegation-Credential) and\nstructurally recognized VC-JWTs; opaque and unrelated bearer tokens do not\ntrigger root lookup. Credential claims still require engine verification,\nincluding issuer, project, holder, expiry and revocation. Cedar evaluation\nerrors on enforcing projects fail closed; shadow mode keeps the structured\ndecision.\n\nInstruct fallback link (#6103): with no policy redirect URL, the Instruct and\nChallenge responses linked to {BaseUrl}/api/v1/bouncer/authorize, which needs\nagent_did and requested_scopes, so every agent that followed it got a 400.\nMcpInstructBuilder.BuildAuthUrl now falls back to the project's connect page,\n{BaseUrl}/connect/{ProjectId}?agent=<agent name, lowercased>, the page the\ndashboard and the DNS gateway send. A policy redirect URL still wins. The\nproject id is percent-encoded as one path segment; an unnamed agent gets\nagent=unknown. The hostname argument is no longer used.\n\nEmbedded engines (#6260): the compiled OpenAI signing key seed is re-stamped\nfrom the live directory (it now expires 2026-10-26) and the lean and Cedar\nengines are rebuilt from the same source. Nothing else changes.\n\nNo behavior change (#6062): PolicyEvaluator takes an optional lock-wait timeout\non its internal constructor, for tests on loaded machines.\n\nAlready shipped in 1.12.0, listed because they merged after its version commit:\n#5926, #5949, #5974, #5983 and #5987.\n\nThis is a minor version, with these public API changes: SupplementaryDetector\nis obsolete-as-error (no adapter in this release calls it);\nSignedStatusListVerifier and StatusList2021 are removed, since the engine now\nverifies status lists; WebMcpReadOnlyOptions.StatusListUrl is removed; and\nStatusIssuerDocuments is added. Code that used the removed members no longer\ncompiles. Update all four packages together; a 1.12.0 or 1.11.9 adapter keeps\nworking on this Core.\nTarget frameworks and third-party dependency versions are unchanged from 1.12.0.\n\nRelease history: https://github.com/Know-That-Ai/checkpoint/blob/main/packages/checkpoint-dotnet/CHANGELOG.md",
